Here is a simple yet effective Java implementation that creates a custom thread-safe stack. It includes methods such as push, pop, peek, isEmpty, and getMin to obtain the minimum element. This stack implementation is generic and uses a separate minStack for constant time retrieval of minimum element. It uses synchronised block for thread-safety and LinkedList for better memory optimization:

```java
import java.util.LinkedList;

public class MinStack<T extends Comparable<T>> {

    private LinkedList<T> stack;
    private LinkedList<T> minStack;

    public MinStack() {
        stack = new LinkedList<>();
        minStack = new LinkedList<>();
    }

    public synchronized void push(T item) {
        if (minStack.isEmpty() || item.compareTo(minStack.peek()) <= 0) {
            minStack.push(item);
        }
        stack.push(item);
    }

    public synchronized T pop() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }
        if (stack.peek().compareTo(minStack.peek()) == 0) {
            minStack.pop();
        }
        return stack.pop();
    }

    public synchronized T peek() {
        return isEmpty() ? null : stack.peek();
    }

    public synchronized T getMin() {
        return minStack.peek();
    }

    public synchronized boolean isEmpty() {
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }
}
```
Please remember: 

1. This stack implementation requires that the type parameter T extends Comparable<T>. It means that types that will be stored in the MinStack must have a natural ordering.

2. The method getMin() returns the "minimum" element in the stack according to their natural ordering. It considers the recently added input(s) to the stack.

3. This implementation uses a wrapper class provided by Java instead of primitive types for Generics.

4. It assumes that null elements will not be stored in the stack.

