It's important to note that PyFlink is a Python library for processing stream data using Apache Flink, and there is currently no direct equivalent in C#. However, the same logic could be achieved using the .NET API for Apache Kafka (Confluent.Kafka), which is a similar platform for processing stream data and connecting to a MySQL database. Note that the logic may look somewhat different because Flink and Kafka have different concepts and APIs. Here is an example for the same logic in Kafka:

```csharp
using Confluent.Kafka;
using System;
using MySql.Data.MySqlClient;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Use Kafka's producer to read from source
        var conf = new ProducerConfig { BootstrapServers = "localhost:9092" };

        using (var p = new ProducerBuilder<Null, string>(conf).Build())
        {
            p.Produce("clickstreamLog", new Message<Null, string> { Value = "/path/to/clickstream_logs" });

            // Commit changes to the Kafka broker
            p.Flush(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));
        }

        // .. apply transformations here..

        // Connect to MySQL sink
        string connStr = "server=localhost;user=root;database=test;port=3306;password=SECRET";

        using (MySqlConnection conn = new MySqlConnection(connStr))
        {
            conn.Open();

            // Insert into MySQL
            string sql = "INSERT INTO output_table (column1, column2) VALUES (@column1, @column2)";

            using (MySqlCommand cmd = new MySqlCommand(sql, conn))
            {
                cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue("@column1", "value1");
                cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue("@column2", "value2");
                cmd.ExecuteNonQuery();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This skeleton code demonstrates how one would build a pipeline to read from a Kafka topic (equivalent of log source), apply some transformation (which is provided according to the specific business logic), and then connect to a MySQL database to write the results. The values that are object-relevant parameters (like `BootstrapServers`, path to the logs, db credentials) should be replaced to fit the actual application environment. Similar to Flink, Kafka is widely used for processing and handling of real-time data feeds.

