Here is the JavaScript function which takes an array and a callback function as parameters and sorts the array using the custom comparison specified in the callback function:

```javascript
function customSort(array, callback) {
    for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
        for (let j = 0; j < array.length - i - 1; j++) {
            if (callback(array[j], array[j + 1]) > 0) {
                // Swap array elements
                let temp = array[j];
                array[j] = array[j + 1];
                array[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
    return array;
}
```

You can use this function for example with a callback function that compares the absolute values of two numbers:

```javascript
const array = [-3, -1, 2, -5, 8];
const callback = function(a, b) {
    if (Math.abs(a) < Math.abs(b)) {
        return -1;
    } else if (Math.abs(a) === Math.abs(b)) {
        return 0;
    } else {
        return 1;
    }
};
console.log(customSort(array, callback)); // expecting [-1, 2, -3, -5, 8]
```

In this example, the array will be sorted based on the absolute values of its elements, not the actual values. This means that -1 will be considered smaller than 2, -3 will be smaller than -2, and so on. You can replace the callback function with any function that provides your custom comparison.

Please note that this is a simple implementation for illustrative purposes and does not optimize for performance. For large arrays, other sorting algorithms (e.g., QuickSort, MergeSort) are recommended. Please also note that JavaScript provides a built-in sort function which accepts a comparison function as an optional parameter. This can greatly simplify the task:

```javascript
array.sort(callback);
```

