According to https://stackoverflow.com/questions/74180422/requestbuilder-does-not-contain-a-definition-for-request a new method is required, which is detailed in https://github.com/microsoftgraph/msgraph-sdk-dotnet/blob/feature/5.0/docs/upgrade-to-v5.md. Please adjust the provided code to fix the error.

You are correct: in **Microsoft Graph .NET SDK v5+**, the `Request()` method is **removed**. You now call the method directly on the builder — for collections, it's usually `.AddAsync(...)`, `.GetAsync(...)`, etc. You do **not** use `.Request()` anymore.

Here's how to correct your code:

### Change this line:
```csharp
await _graphClient.Users[UtilityAccountUsername].Calendars[calendarId].Events.Request().AddAsync(newEvent).ConfigureAwait(false);
```

### To this (v5+ style):
```csharp
await _graphClient.Users[UtilityAccountUsername].Calendars[calendarId].Events.PostAsync(newEvent).ConfigureAwait(false);
```

#### **Explanation:**
- **V4 (Old)**:  
  `.Events.Request().AddAsync(newEvent)`
- **V5+ (New)**:  
  `.Events.PostAsync(newEvent)`

> The new SDK now uses HTTP verb-named methods, so you call `.PostAsync` to create (add) new events.

---

## **Full Corrected Method**
Here is the corrected event handler method in context:

```csharp
private async void submitBtn_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    if (_graphClient != null)
    {
        string calendarId = "[OUTAGE-CALENDAR - ID]";
        var newEvent = new Event
        {
            Subject = eventNameTextBox.Text,
            Start = new DateTimeTimeZone { DateTime = startDateTimePicker.Value.ToString("o"), TimeZone = "UTC" },
            End = new DateTimeTimeZone { DateTime = endDateTimePicker.Value.ToString("o"), TimeZone = "UTC" },
            Body = new ItemBody
            {
                ContentType = BodyType.Text,
                Content = $"Username: {_selectedMailItem.Sender.Name}\nNote: {eventNoteTextBox.Text}\n\nEmail Text: {emailContentTextBox.Text}"
            }
        };

        // UPDATED LINE FOR GRAPH SDK v5+
        await _graphClient.Users[UtilityAccountUsername].Calendars[calendarId].Events.PostAsync(newEvent).ConfigureAwait(false);

        MessageBox.Show("Outage event has been added to the shared calendar.");
        ((Form)this.TopLevelControl).Close();
    }
    else
    {
        MessageBox.Show("Error: Unable to add the outage event. Please try again.");
    }
}
```

## **Docs Reference**
- [Microsoft Graph SDK v5 Upgrading Guide](https://github.com/microsoftgraph/msgraph-sdk-dotnet/blob/feature/5.0/docs/upgrade-to-v5.md)
- [Calendar Events SDK Reference](https://github.com/microsoftgraph/msgraph-sdk-dotnet/blob/dev/docs/overview.md) (search for `.PostAsync` example)

---

## **Summary**

You must **replace any use of `.Request()` and old-style methods** with the new HTTP verb-named methods in Microsoft Graph SDK v5+, for example:

| Action/Verb | Old SDK v4 pattern                   | New SDK v5+ pattern             |
|-------------|--------------------------------------|---------------------------------|
| Create      | `.Events.Request().AddAsync(e)`      | `.Events.PostAsync(e)`          |
| Read        | `.Events.Request().GetAsync()`       | `.Events.GetAsync()`            |
| Update      | `.Events[id].Request().UpdateAsync(e)`| `.Events[id].PatchAsync(e)`     |
| Delete      | `.Events[id].Request().DeleteAsync()`| `.Events[id].DeleteAsync()`     |

**Just use `.PostAsync`, `.GetAsync`, etc. directly.**

---

If you make this change, your code will work with the new SDK!

